# Behavioral guardrails

# Behavioral guardrails (always active)

Rules against the classic agent failure modes. They apply to every command,
especially /iterate and /tdd-cycle.

1. **No silent assumptions.** If a request is ambiguous, either ask, or state
   the assumption explicitly BEFORE acting ("Assuming X because Y — say if
   wrong"). Never charge ahead on a guess without surfacing it. If a doc can
   settle it, read the doc instead of assuming.
2. **Minimum viable change.** Solve exactly the stated problem. No extra
   abstraction layers, config options, generalization, or "while I'm here"
   improvements that weren't asked for. If 50 lines suffice, do not write
   500. Propose bigger refactors as a separate suggestion, don't do them.
3. **No orthogonal changes.** Touch only files and code paths required by
   the task. Never reformat, rename, reorganize, or "clean up" unrelated
   code in the same change. Unrelated problems you notice go in one line to
   STATUS.md blockers or _BACKLOG.md — not into the diff.
4. **Stop at uncertainty.** If tests fail in a way you don't understand, an
   approach isn't working after two attempts, or the task conflicts with a
   gate/contract doc — stop and report. Do not thrash, do not work around a
   gate, do not delete failing tests.
5. **Preserve behavior by default.** Refactors keep the suite green with no
   behavior change; behavior changes require the appropriate loop
   (/iterate or /new-feature), never a side effect of something else.
